---
import ContentPage from '../layouts/ContentPage.astro';
import SectionHeader from '../components/marketing/SectionHeader.astro';
import PackGroupCard from '../components/marketing/PackGroupCard.astro';
import PrintableBand from '../components/marketing/PrintableBand.astro';
import HospitalQuestions from '../components/marketing/HospitalQuestions.astro';
import MomKit from '../components/illustrations/MomKit.astro';
import { ROUTES } from '../config/site';
import { MOM_GROUPS, DOCUMENT_GROUP } from '../data/content/checklists';
import { MOM_FAQS } from '../data/content/faqs';

const contents = [
  { label: 'What to pack', href: '#what-to-pack' },
  { label: 'Two bags, not one', href: '#two-bags' },
  { label: 'Documents', href: '#documents' },
  { label: 'Ask your hospital', href: '#hospital' },
  { label: 'FAQs', href: '#faq' },
];
---

<ContentPage
  routeKey="mom"
  h1="Hospital Bag Checklist for Mom"
  lead="Your hospital bag should make labor, delivery and recovery a little easier. This mom hospital bag checklist covers what to pack for the labor room, for your hospital stay and for the day you go home."
  groups={[...MOM_GROUPS, DOCUMENT_GROUP]}
  faqs={MOM_FAQS}
  related={['baby', 'dad', 'csection']}
  contents={contents}
  ctaTitle="Build your bag in a couple of minutes"
  ctaLead="Answer a few questions about your delivery plan and stay, and get a mom checklist with the right quantities for you."
>
  <MomKit slot="illustration" class="w-full max-w-md lg:ml-auto" />

  <section id="what-to-pack" class="section">
    <div class="container-page">
      <div class="prose-clay mx-auto max-w-[45rem]">
        <h2>What to pack in your hospital bag</h2>
        <p>
          It helps to think about your bag in three moments rather than as one long list:
          <strong>labor</strong>, <strong>the stay afterwards</strong> and
          <strong>going home</strong>. Those three moments need quite different things, and
          separating them is the fastest way to stop the list feeling endless.
        </p>
        <p>
          During labor you want very little — something loose to wear, warm socks, water within
          reach and your documents already handed over. Afterwards you want comfort and practical
          clothing that works for feeding and recovery. On the day you leave, you want one outfit
          that fits the body you have that week, not the one you had nine months ago.
        </p>
        <p>
          A quick note on quantities: the numbers below suit a typical two to three night stay. If
          your hospital expects a longer stay, or you are planning a cesarean, add a spare set or
          two rather than doubling everything.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-14 grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {MOM_GROUPS.map((group, index) => <PackGroupCard group={group} index={index + 1} />)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="two-bags" class="section bg-surface-soft">
    <div class="container-page">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="A small trick"
        title="Pack two bags, not one."
        lead="Almost every parent who has done this once says the same thing: one big bag is harder to live with than two small ones."
      />

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="card-feature bg-brand-coral text-on-coral">
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] opacity-75">Bag one</span>
          <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-2xl font-medium">The labor bag</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed opacity-90">
            Small enough to carry in one hand and keep beside you in the delivery room. Documents,
            one loose thing to wear, socks, water, lip balm, a hair tie and a long charger. That is
            genuinely most of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card-feature bg-brand-lavender text-on-lavender">
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] opacity-75">Bag two</span>
          <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-2xl font-medium">The stay bag</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed opacity-90">
            Clothes, underwear, maternity pads, toiletries and your going-home outfit. This one can
            wait in the car and be brought up once you have been admitted and moved to a room.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-clay mx-auto mt-14 max-w-[45rem]">
        <h3>What to wear during labor and after birth</h3>
        <p>
          For labor, most people choose a loose nightdress or an oversized old T-shirt they will not
          mind ruining. Hospitals will offer you a gown too, so this is about comfort rather than
          necessity. Warm socks come up again and again — feet get cold, and the floors are hard.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the days after, look for two features: <strong>front opening</strong> and
          <strong>a high waistband</strong>. Front-opening pyjamas make feeding far easier than
          anything you have to pull up or over your head, and a high waist is more comfortable
          whether you have a vaginal birth or a cesarean. Dark colours are practical for obvious
          reasons.
        </p>
        <p>
          For going home, pack the maternity sizing you have been living in. It is completely normal
          to still look around six months pregnant on discharge day, and soft leggings with a loose
          top will feel much better than the jeans you optimistically packed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="documents" class="section">
    <div class="container-page">
      <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,0.85fr)_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16">
        <SectionHeader
          eyebrow="Paperwork"
          title="Documents to bring to the hospital"
          lead="This is the section worth sorting first, because it is the one thing you cannot improvise at 3am. Put everything in one folder, and make sure your partner knows exactly where that folder lives."
        />
        <PackGroupCard group={DOCUMENT_GROUP} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="hospital" class="section bg-surface-soft">
    <div class="container-page">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="Before you pack"
        title="Ask your hospital what they provide"
        lead="Maternity pads, towels and toiletries are the items that vary most between hospitals. A short phone call can save you carrying two packs you never open."
      />
      <div class="mt-10">
        <HospitalQuestions limit={6} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container-page">
      <PrintableBand />
      <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-muted">
        Packing for the baby too? See the
        <a
          href={ROUTES.baby.path}
          class="text-link"
          >baby hospital bag checklist</a
        >, or the
        <a
          href={ROUTES.momAndBaby.path}
          class="text-link"
          >combined list for mom, baby and dad</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ContentPage>
